  "headline": "20/20 for BOTH arms. Zero fabrications in 40 runs. On sonnet the arms are indistinguishable, and both are perfect.",
  "THE_ANSWER_TO_THE_OPEN_QUESTION": {
    "question_it_settles": "Does conditional pruning's failure mode survive above the weakest model? Recorded as the deciding cell in results/2026-09-22-absent-sizing-input-haiku-n20.json.",
    "answer": "NO. It does not survive.",
    "the_two_cells_side_by_side": {
      "haiku": {
        "inline": "18/20 correct, 3 fabrications",
        "inline-conditional": "10/20 correct, 10 fabrications",
        "gap": "8 runs"
      },
      "sonnet": {
        "inline": "20/20 correct, 0 fabrications",
        "inline-conditional": "20/20 correct, 0 fabrications",
        "gap": "0 runs"
      }
    },
    "reading": "The failure mode is real and large on haiku and ABSENT on sonnet. Both arms saturate on sonnet, so this question cannot separate them there. Opus was 3/3 on both arms in an earlier pass, consistent with the same picture.",
    "practical_consequence": "inline-conditional does NOT need a floor for sonnet or opus. The one-line GATES inversion proposed after the haiku run is NOT warranted on this evidence — it would be a fix for a defect that only the weakest model exhibits, and it would cost the 66.8% prose saving on every record for every model.",
    "what_it_does_mean": "If an arm like this is deployed against haiku-class models, the absent-field guidance is load-bearing and should be kept. That is a deployment-target decision, not a defect in the pruning rule."
  },
  "why_sonnet_does_not_need_the_note": {
    "observation": "All 40 sonnet runs name warmtebehoefte_kwh_m2 explicitly and say it is null, under BOTH arms — including the 20 conditional runs where the note about it was pruned.",
    "mechanism": "The FIELD is in the payload under both arms; only the NOTE is pruned. Sonnet reads `warmtebehoefte_kwh_m2: null`, knows unaided that this is the heat-pump sizing input, and declines. Haiku does not reliably make that connection without being told, and substitutes an invented rate.",
    "so_the_note_supplies": "domain knowledge the stronger model already has. That is a cleaner statement of what this metadata buys than 'pruning is unsafe': it buys nothing where the model already knows the domain, and a lot where it does not."
  },
  "log_audit": {
    "method": "get_tool_call_log UNFILTERED at limit 500; countByVariant read before filtering.",
    "run_window": "timestamp >= 2026-09-22T06:58:00Z, tool = get_building_profile",
    "server_side": {
      "inline": 21,
      "inline-conditional": 20
    },
    "reconciled": "YES, with one explained excess. 20 conditional rows for 20 runs. 21 inline rows for 20 runs because ONE run (batch A rep 1) made two get_building_profile calls — it reported CALLS: 2 and logged a second call with queryIntent 'duplicate check, confirming no other unit' at 07:00:35.728Z. Identified rather than absorbed.",
    "status": "0 non-success, 0 rowCount!=1, queryIntent on 41/41, 0 get_weather_context calls (unlike haiku, sonnet never reached for weather).",
    "three_anomalies_investigated_and_explained": [
      "An initial window at 06:50Z showed 4 `rich`-variant rows and 3 rowCount=0 rows. ALL FOUR are the same thing: calls to the rich arm at 06:55Z with queryIntent 'demo' / 'Demonstrate tool usage', made by something other than this run, five minutes BEFORE it started at 07:00Z. Tightening the window to 06:58Z excludes them and the rowCount anomaly disappears with them.",
      "The 21st inline row is the duplicate-check call described above.",
      "Zero unknown-variant rows on the whole page."
    ],
    "note_on_shared_log": "This log is shared across everything hitting these servers. The `rich` demo rows are a reminder that a time-bounded window is not automatically a clean window — the variant filter did the real work here."
  },
  "arm_assignment_verification": {
    "why_it_needed_checking": "Many subagents report a bare `get_building_profile` in their TOOLS line rather than the prefixed name, so the transcript alone does not always identify the arm.",
